mode: "100644"
text: "# Scoring & Selection\n\nGood hooks come from volume plus ruthless selection, not from nailing the first attempt. This is\nthe method and the rubric.\n\n## The method: generate many, keep one\n\n1. **Generate 5–10** candidate hooks for the piece, deliberately spanning *different\n   mechanisms* (curiosity, contrarian, specificity, story, stakes, question…). Forcing variety\n   is what surfaces the strong angle you wouldn't have found by polishing the first idea.\n2. **Score** each against the rubric below.\n3. **Keep the top 1–2.** Most hooks you write should die — that's the process working, not\n   failing.\n4. **Refine** the winner for format and voice.\n\nWriting one hook and shipping it is the single most common reason hooks underperform.\n\n## The rubric\n\nScore each candidate 0–2 on each dimension (0 = no, 1 = partly, 2 = yes). The winner is rarely\nthe cleverest — it's the one that clears every line.\n\n| Dimension | The question |\n|---|---|\n| **Gap** | Does it open a real curiosity/tension/stakes gap a stranger needs to close? |\n| **Specificity** | Is there a concrete number, detail, or claim — not a vague tease? |\n| **Truth** | Does the content actually pay this off? (No bait-and-switch.) |\n| **Fit** | Does it land inside the format's cutoff / first 3 seconds? |\n| **Voice** | Does it sound like them — fingerprint hit, \"never\" list respected? |\n| **Pull** | Read cold: would a stranger *need* the next beat? |\n\n**Truth and Pull are pass/fail gates.** A hook that scores high but overpromises (Truth = 0) is\ndisqualified no matter how compelling — it wins the tap and loses the trust, and the bounce\nhurts reach. A hook that doesn't make you need the next beat (Pull = 0) isn't a hook yet,\nwhatever else it does.\n\n## Anti-patterns — delete these on sight\n\n- **Throat-clearing:** \"In today's video…\", \"So I've been thinking about…\", \"Let's talk about\n  ___.\" The hook is buried after the warm-up.\n- **Greetings:** \"Hey everyone!\", \"Happy Monday!\" — attention donated to the scroll.\n- **Describing the asset:** \"Here's a photo of our team at the event.\" The eye already sees it.\n- **Vague tease / empty curiosity:** \"You won't believe this.\" No real gap, no specificity.\n- **Overpromise / clickbait:** a sensational claim the content can't deliver. Burns trust and\n  bounces.\n- **Giving away the payoff:** the first line answers its own question, so there's no reason to\n  continue. Hold something back.\n- **Too broad an address:** \"Attention everyone…\" — the opposite of self-relevant.\n- **Template-by-rote:** a swipe-file formula in an obviously borrowed voice. Use the *mechanism*,\n  write it fresh in their voice.\n\n## A fast scoring pass (in practice)\n\nFor each candidate, read only the hook (cut to the cutoff) and ask in order:\n1. Would a stranger need the next beat? *(no → cut it)*\n2. Is the content going to deliver this? *(no → cut it)*\n3. Is it specific? *(no → sharpen or cut)*\n4. Does it sound like them? *(no → rewrite in voice)*\n\nWhat survives all four is your shortlist. Recommend the strongest and say why in one line.\n"
